.TH comm "" "Command"
.SH NAME
comm \- print lines common to two files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcomm [\-123] \fIfile1 file2\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
comm -- print lines common to two files
Usage:	comm [ -123 ] file1 file2
Options:
	-1	Suppress column 1
	-2	Suppress column 2
	-3	Suppress column 3
Column 1 has lines unique to 'file1'; column 2 has lines unique to 'file2';
column 3 has lines common to both files.  Both files should be sorted.
.HE
.B comm
reads
.I file1
and
.I file2
line by line and compares them, printing each line in one of three
columns separated by tabs.
Column 1 holds lines found only in
.IR file1 ,
column 2 holds lines found only in
.IR file2 ,
and column 3 holds lines found in both.
Both files should already be sorted into the same order, since
.B comm
merges rather than searches them.
Either
.I file1
or
.I file2
(but not both) may be
.B \-
to mean the standard input.
.IP \fB\-1\fR
Do not print column 1 (lines unique to
.IR file1 ).
.IP \fB\-2\fR
Do not print column 2 (lines unique to
.IR file2 ).
.IP \fB\-3\fR
Do not print column 3 (lines common to both files).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cmp ,
.B sort
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B comm
exits 1 if either file cannot be opened, or if it is not given
exactly two file arguments.
